    "description": "Digital publishing connects editorial work with electronic formats, distribution platforms, and reading or listening software. Iranian services such as Taaghche and Fidibo illustrate how Persian-language books, audio, and periodicals reach audiences through platform-mediated access.",
    "tags": ["Digital publishing", "Persian books", "E-books", "Audiobooks", "Digital magazines", "Reading platforms"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:52:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:52:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Digital Publishing in Iran: Formats, Platforms and Access",
      "description": "Explore Persian digital books, audiobooks, magazines, publishing platforms, and the distinction between content production and reader access."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "publishing-chain",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From an edited work to a usable publication",
        "content": "Digital publishing involves more than placing a document online. Taaghche, an Iranian reading and listening platform, describes its role as connecting publishers with readers while preparing supplied content for digital use. Its account separates the publisher’s work from platform tasks such as technical optimization, presentation, discovery, and reader experience. This is the company’s description of its own workflow, rather than an independently audited account of the whole industry. [s1]\n\nThat distinction provides a useful introduction to the field. Editorial production determines the work being published; technical preparation determines how it can be read or heard; distribution determines how an audience obtains access. A weakness at any stage can limit the usefulness of the finished publication. The platform’s account shows why digital distribution still depends on coordination with publishers. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "formats",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Books, audio, and periodicals serve different needs",
        "content": "Taaghche’s frequently asked questions distinguish EPUB books from PDF publications. EPUB is used for text that can adapt to the reading screen, whereas PDF preserves a page arrangement. The service explains that preserving layout matters for many magazines and newspapers. This makes the choice of format an editorial and usability decision: continuous prose and a carefully composed magazine spread do not necessarily benefit from the same presentation. [s2]\n\nFidibo, another Iranian digital reading service, lists electronic books, audiobooks, magazines, and podcasts within its offering. These categories show that a publishing platform may distribute both written and spoken material. They do not make all audio equivalent: an audiobook and a podcast remain distinct editorial forms even when a service offers both. [s3]\n\nThe practical question for a reader is therefore what the particular edition provides. A platform’s broad catalogue description cannot establish the format, navigation, or availability of every title. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "persian-reading",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Persian reading requires deliberate technical preparation",
        "content": "EPUB supports an explicit right-to-left page progression setting. The W3C specification distinguishes that publication-level progression from direction and presentation within individual content documents. For Persian publishing, this means that choosing a supported file format is only the beginning: reading order and local layout still need to be prepared and checked. [s4]\n\nAccessibility is another separate concern. The specification connects EPUB accessibility with web accessibility principles and a dedicated EPUB accessibility specification. A publication’s digital existence therefore does not, by itself, establish that it works well with assistive technology. These standards describe technical capabilities and requirements; they do not certify that the Iranian platforms or individual editions discussed here implement them fully. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "access-and-publishing",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Publishing relationships and reader access",
        "content": "Taaghche’s published terms say that it contracts with licensed publishers and requires the relevant permissions for supplied content. Authors, translators, and narrators wishing to publish through the service are directed to work through a publisher rather than contract directly with the platform. This documents one service’s stated participation model; it should not be generalized into a universal description of Iranian digital publishing. [s5]\n\nThe same terms distinguish access within the service from unrestricted possession of a transferable file. Purchased books are described as remaining in the account archive, while use is restricted to Taaghche’s reading applications. The terms also describe downloading for offline reading. Offline access and permission to redistribute a file are consequently different matters. [s5]\n\nFidibo describes both individual purchases and its FidiPlus subscription offering. A purchase model and a subscription model are different routes to access; neither description alone establishes that every title is included under identical conditions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "branches-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Exploring the field",
        "content": "The branches of this section follow different parts of the publishing process. E-books, audiobooks, and digital magazines concern publication forms. Content publishing platforms concern distribution and access. Translation services and writing tools concern work that contributes to preparing content. This division follows the site’s taxonomy and should not be read as a claim that each branch has the same commercial structure.\n\nThe inspected services offer concrete entry points into Persian digital publishing, but their descriptions are not a census. Platform catalogue totals would need dates, definitions, and treatment of duplicate editions before they could support national comparisons. The most useful starting point is the particular work and edition: its format, publisher, access conditions, and reading or listening experience. [s1][s2][s3][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s5"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
